DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       ddeelluusseerr  and ddeellggrroouupp remove users and groups from the system according
       to   command   line   options   and   configuration   information    in
       _/_e_t_c_/_d_e_l_u_s_e_r_._c_o_n_f and _/_e_t_c_/_a_d_d_u_s_e_r_._c_o_n_f.

       They  are  friendlier  front ends to the uusseerrddeell and ggrroouuppddeell programs,
       removing the home directory as option or even all files on  the  system
       owned  by  the  user  to be removed, running a custom script, and other
       features.

       For a full list and explanations of all options, see the  OPTIONS  sec‐
       tion.

       ddeelluusseerr and ddeellggrroouupp can be run in one of three modes:


   RReemmoovvee aa uusseerr
       If  called with one non-option argument and without the ----ggrroouupp option,
       ddeelluusseerr will remove a non-system user.

       By default, ddeelluusseerr will remove the user without removing the home  di‐
       rectory,  the  mail spool or any other files on the system owned by the
       user.  Removing the home directory and mail spool can be achieved using
       the ----rreemmoovvee--hhoommee option.

       The  ----rreemmoovvee--aallll--ffiilleess option removes all files on the system owned by
       the user.  Note that if you activate both  options  ----rreemmoovvee--hhoommee  will
       have  no  additional effect because all files including the home direc‐
       tory and mail spool are already covered by the  ----rreemmoovvee--aallll--ffiilleess  op‐
       tion.

       If  you  want to backup all files before deleting them you can activate
       the ----bbaacckkuupp option which will create a file _u_s_e_r_n_a_m_e_._t_a_r_(_._g_z_|_._b_z_2_)  in
       the directory specified by the ----bbaacckkuupp--ttoo option.

       By  default,  the backup archive is compressed with ggzziipp(1).  To change
       this, the ----bbaacckkuupp--ssuuffffiixx option can be set to any suffix supported  by
       ttaarr ----aauuttoo--ccoommpprreessss (e.g. .gz, .bz2, .xz).

       ddeelluusseerr will refuse to remove the root account.

       If  the ----ssyysstteemm option is given on the command line, the delete opera‐
       tion is actually executed only if the user  is  a  system  user.   This
       avoids  accidentally  deleting  non-system users.  Additionally, if the
       user does not exist, no error value is returned.  Debian package  main‐
       tainer scripts may use this flag to remove system users or groups while
       ignoring the case where the removal already occurred.


   RReemmoovvee aa ggrroouupp
       If ddeelluusseerr is called with the ----ggrroouupp  option, or ddeellggrroouupp is called, a
       group will be removed.  The primary group of an existing user cannot be
       removed.  If the option ----oonnllyy--iiff--eemmppttyy is given, the  group  won't  be
       removed if it has any members left.

       The  ----ssyysstteemm  option adds the same functionality as for users, respec‐
       tively.


